Once a repository is selected, the Branch Listing feature displays all its branches along with important details like:

Branch name

Age in months

Last committer

And the pull request status and the jira id

To make this easier to manage, I’ve included a Filtering option. You can filter branches by:

Age—say, older than X months

Branch type like feature, master, or release

And by pull request status—such as merged, no PR, open, or declined

Optionally, you can also pull in detailed PR Information for each branch.

you can also get pr information for all the record . but its an api call it it increases the loading time. so i made this check box uncheced by default


Next is Bulk Selection, which allows selecting multiple branches—either all or specific ones—for deletion.

Once you’ve reviewed the list, you can use the Branch Deletion feature to remove them directly from the remote repository using the Git CLI—safely and easily.

If you want the report.  you can export branch data to CSV, 





So, how does this help?

It saves time by automating the cleanup of stale branches.

It reduces clutter, keeping your repositories tidy and focused.

It improves collaboration by ensuring that only relevant branches are visible, reducing confusion for your team.

And finally, it supports auditing with CSV exports that track repository health.

That’s Git Branch Cleaner—a simple, effective tool to make Git branch management easier.
